@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — Theravāda Buddhism
#
# The tradition with the closest thing to a graded phenomenology of the
# bodily rapture the modern draft calls a wave, and with the strongest
# counterweight: it catalogues the capacities and forbids their display in
# the same corpus.
#

pmt:Theravada a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Theravāda"@en, "Theravāda"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Pāli canon and its commentarial literature, of which the Visuddhimagga of Buddhaghosa is the systematising handbook."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Visuddhimagga citations name the translation they were read from, and Dīgha Nikāya 2 was reopened for this purpose. For Majjhima Nikāya 118, Dīgha Nikāya 11 and Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8 the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Pali Canon, in the Dīgha and Majjhima Nikāyas, the Vinaya and the Jātaka; Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya, pmw:MajjhimaNikaya, pmw:VinayaPitaka, pmw:Jataka, pmw:Anagatavamsa ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#
# Added on 2026-09-21 with the first passage from outside the Visuddhimagga
# group. The file was not migrated before; AGENTS.md requires the migration of
# anyone who touches a tradition file, and it is the cheap half of this change.
#

pmw:Visuddhimagga a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga"@en, "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga"@de ;
    skos:note "The systematising handbook of the tradition and the source of most of this file. Read in Ñāṇamoli's English of 1956."@en .

pmw:DighaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Dīgha Nikāya"@en, "Dīgha Nikāya"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at suttas 1, 2 and 11. For sutta 1 the edition is recoverable, because the Mahāsīla was read at the wording in this run: T. W. Rhys Davids, Dialogues of the Buddha, Part I, Sacred Books of the Buddhists vol. II, London 1899, pp. 16-26. For suttas 2 and 11 the edition is not recoverable and prima-materia#45 records that."@en .

pmw:MajjhimaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Majjhima Nikāya"@en, "Majjhima Nikāya"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at sutta 118. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:VinayaPitaka a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Vinaya Piṭaka"@en, "Vinaya Piṭaka"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at Cūḷavagga V.8, and the edition is now recoverable. The passage was read at the wording on 2026-09-24 in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids and Hermann Oldenberg, Vinaya Texts, Part III, The Kullavagga IV-XII, Sacred Books of the East vol. XX, Oxford 1885, pp. 78-81, which is public domain and reachable by the regular route. That closes the Vinaya half of prima-materia#45; the Dīgha Nikāya 11 half is still open."@en .

pmw:Anagatavamsa a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Anāgatavaṃsa, the history of what is to come"@en, "Anāgatavaṃsa, die Geschichte des Kommenden"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-24 at the passage on the five disappearances only. The text read is the recension Minayeff marked B, a mixed prose and verse copy of a Rangoon manuscript, printed by him in the Journal of the Pali Text Society for 1886, pp. 33-36, and rendered into English by Henry Clarke Warren, Buddhism in Translations, Harvard 1896, §102, pp. 481-486. The remainder of the work, which is about the next Buddha, was not read. The recension matters and is named because Minayeff printed more than one and they do not agree."@en .

pmw:Jataka a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Jātaka"@en, "Jātaka"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-21 at story 49 only, in the English of Robert Chalmers under E. B. Cowell's editorship, volume I of 1895. The remaining five hundred and forty-six stories were not read. All six volumes are public domain and reachable by the regular route, which makes this the cheapest continuation for anything the canon states as doctrine and the Jātaka reports as a case."@en .

#
# Practices
#

pmp:Anapanasati a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:MajjhimaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Ānāpānasati"@en, "Ānāpānasati"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mindfulness of in-breath and out-breath, set out as a sixteen-step progression in the discourse and as a meditation subject in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Majjhima Nikāya 118", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"@en, "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor"@de ;
    skos:note "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:Piti ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmp:BrahmaviharaBhavana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Cultivation of the divine abidings"@en, "Kultivierung der göttlichen Verweilungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate raising and sustaining of a chosen affective state as a meditation subject in its own right."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IX (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Capacities and states
#

pmc:Piti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Pīti"@en, "Pīti"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rapture as a bodily felt state, graded in the handbook into five degrees of increasing pervasion."@en ;
    skos:note "The five degrees are khuddikā, khaṇikā, okkantikā, ubbegā and pharaṇā."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:KhuddikaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Minor rapture"@en, "Geringe Verzückung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The least of the five degrees of rapture, described as able to raise the hairs of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage that attests piloerection as a recognised concomitant of a meditative state, not as its object."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:UbbegaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Uplifting rapture"@en, "Erhebungsverzückung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of the five degrees of rapture, described as strong enough to lift the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Manomayakaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Mind-made body"@en, "Geistgemachter Körper"@de ;
    skos:definition "A second body, formed and complete in its faculties, said to be produced from this one; the classical form of the claim the modern draft calls exteriorisation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Dibbacakkhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Divine eye"@en, "Göttliches Auge"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sight beyond the range of the physical eye."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Dibbasota a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Divine ear"@en, "Göttliches Ohr"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Cetopariyanana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge encompassing another mind"@en, "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins"@de ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the state of another consciousness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Steps a prerequisite claim points at
#

pmc:Sila a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Virtue"@en, "Sittlichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three trainings, on which the handbook is built as a commentary on the verse about the tangle."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Samadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration"@en, "Sammlung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second of the three trainings, named in the handbook as the condition for the six direct knowledges."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:FourthJhana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The fourth absorption"@en, "Die vierte Vertiefung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The absorption from which the handbook has the supernormal powers proceed, and which it calls their natural plane."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Iddhividha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The kinds of supernormal power"@en, "Die Arten übernatürlicher Kraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Transformation by supernormal power, the first of the direct knowledges treated in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmp:FourteenfoldMastery a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Control of the mind in fourteen ways"@en, "Beherrschung des Geistes in vierzehn Weisen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A numbered course of mastery over the meditation objects, among them the order of the kasiṇa and its reverse, skipping the absorption, skipping the object, and the definition of the object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.2 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Prerequisite claims
#
# The strongest passage in the whole collection stands here. The handbook
# does not merely order the training; it says outright that the attempt
# cannot even be begun without what precedes it.
#

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourteenfoldMastery a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Supernormal power presupposes the fourteenfold mastery"@en, "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierzehnfache Beherrschung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FourteenfoldMastery ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that it is not possible for a meditator even to begin to accomplish transformation by supernormal power without having first completed the development of control of the mind in those fourteen ways." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"@en, "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"@en, "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Dibbacakkhu, pmc:Dibbasota, pmc:Cetopariyanana, pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration presupposes virtue"@en, "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Sila ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:FiveHindrances a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The five hindrances"@en, "Die fünf Hemmnisse"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt, whose abandoning the discourse describes immediately before entry into the first absorption."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" .

pmc:FourAbsorptions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The four absorptions"@en, "Die vier Vertiefungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A graded series in which each stage is reached by letting go of a factor still present in the one before it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:JhanaPresupposesAbandoningTheHindrances a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Absorption presupposes the abandoning of the hindrances"@en, "Die Vertiefung setzt das Aufgeben der Hemmnisse voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FiveHindrances ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The discourse places the abandoning of the five immediately before entry into the first absorption in a fixed formula, without stating at that place what befalls one who enters otherwise."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" .

pmc:AbsorptionsAreGraded a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Each absorption presupposes the one before it"@en, "Jede Vertiefung setzt die vorhergehende voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The series is strictly graded in both canon and handbook, each stage defined by what it has let go of. No passage was found that forbids skipping in so many words, which is why this is recorded as an order and not as a necessity."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MajjhimaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"@en, "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Majjhima Nikāya 118" .

#
# A dispute
#

pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "How high the threshold of absorption stands"@en, "Wie hoch die Schwelle der Vertiefung liegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what is required for absorption. The discourses describe it as reached in the ordinary course of the training; the handbook sets a far higher bar, remarking that only one in a hundred or a thousand accomplishes it."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement bears directly on every prerequisite in this file that runs through the fourth absorption. If the handbook's threshold is the right one, the capacities it makes conditional on that absorption are conditional on something very few reach; if the discourses' is, they are not."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Cautions
#
# Both are load-bearing for any project that collects capacity claims: one
# refuses the display of powers as a means of persuasion, the other names
# the states of practice that are mistaken for its completion.
#

pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya, pmw:VinayaPitaka ;
    rdfs:label "Display of powers refused"@en, "Zurschaustellung der Kräfte verweigert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, and holds only instruction to be reliable; the display of power for the sake of persuasion is refused as something to be ashamed of."@en ;
    skos:note "The Vinaya rule against displaying superhuman states to laypeople stands alongside it."@en ;
    skos:note "The Vinaya place was read at the wording on 2026-09-24 and its edition is now named; see pmw:VinayaPitaka. What stands there is a case and not a doctrine. A merchant hangs a sandalwood bowl on a pole and offers it to any recluse or brahman who is a saint and possessed of the power; six teachers of other schools ask for it in turn and are told to fetch it down themselves; an elder rises into the air, takes it and goes three times round the town; a crowd follows him shouting; and the Blessed One, hearing the shouting, rebukes him in the terms the definition gives, forbids the display before laypeople on pain of a wrongdoing, and orders the bowl broken up and ground to powder for eye ointment. The comparison drawn is with a woman who displays herself for a coin."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things in that account bear on this file and were not in it. The offer was made to the holder of the power by a layman, and taken up by six named teachers before any monk touched it, so the act forbidden is one the surrounding world expected. And the ground given for the prohibition is neither that the power is unreal nor that it is dangerous: it is that the display will not convert the unconverted and will turn back the converted. The rule is about what the display does to an audience."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Dibbacakkhu, pmc:Dibbasota, pmc:Cetopariyanana, pmc:Manomayakaya, pmc:AkasenaGamana ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 11 (Kevaṭṭa Sutta)", "Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8, Vin ii.110-112, in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids and Hermann Oldenberg, Vinaya Texts, Part III, Sacred Books of the East vol. XX, Oxford 1885, pp. 78-81" .

pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The imperfections of insight"@en, "Die Unvollkommenheiten der Einsicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ten states arising in practice, among them luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure and resolve, which are not defilements in themselves but become obstacles when taken as proof of attainment."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook illustrates the error with the monk who held himself awakened for sixty years. This is the tradition's own answer to the question of how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Piti ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Placement on the awareness axes. Compiler acts: the handbook says "from the
# fourth absorption", it does not say "withdrawn absorption". The coordinate
# is ours and is entered as ours.
#

pmc:IddhiIsExercisedInWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The supernormal powers are exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en, "Die übernatürlichen Kräfte werden in abgezogener Vertiefung ausgeübt"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbook assigns the powers to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption their natural plane. The absorption is one of the four described as awake and turned from the senses to a single object, which is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the exercise, not of the training that leads to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:ManomayakayaIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The mind-made body is a displaced locus"@en, "Der geistgemachte Körper ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Manomayakaya ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situationGround "The source has a second body, complete in its faculties, produced from this one and going where this one does not. That is the criterion for the displaced value; whether anything of the sort occurs is a separate question and not this one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" .

pmc:SamadhiIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Die Sammlung liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbook names concentration as the condition of the direct knowledges and develops it as the four absorptions, which it describes as awake and turned from the senses to a single object. The coordinate is read off that development and not off the word itself; concentration in a weaker sense would not carry it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The day-choosing refused, and refused with a case
#

pmc:TheravadaNakkhatta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Jataka ;
    rdfs:label "The fool may watch for lucky days, and luck shall always miss"@en, "Der Tor mag auf günstige Tage achten, und das Glück wird ihm immer entgehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The forty-ninth story, told twice, once of the present and once of the past, and both times as a run with an outcome. A family has fixed a wedding day and afterwards asks its household ascetic whether the stars are favourable for it. The ascetic, piqued at having been consulted second, answers that they are not and that great misfortune would follow; the family stays at home; the other side, having prepared everything, marries the girl to somebody else; and the party that obeyed the constellation goes home with nothing. A wise man passing through puts the argument in one stanza: the fool may watch for lucky days, yet luck shall always miss; it is luck itself that is luck's own star, and what can mere stars achieve."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes this worth a node rather than a proverb is that the refusal is carried by a case and not by a doctrine. The transmission does not argue that constellations have no power; it reports an occasion on which the rule was followed exactly and the follower lost the thing he wanted, and it names the mechanism of the loss — an offended interpreter with a motive. The same corpus that supplies this puts nakṣatra prognostication among the low arts elsewhere, which is the doctrinal form; this is the other form, and it is the one a protocol can use."@en ;
    skos:note "The motive matters and is stated in the text twice. The ascetic knows the stars are indifferent and says otherwise because he was asked second. A transmission that explains a false reading of the calendar by the reader's pique has located the failure in a party — which is exactly the location the holding's generalization about calendar-bound operations says these transmissions do not use. It does not refute that statement, because this is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar; it is one that reports somebody else's binding. The difference is recorded here so that a later run does not count it on the wrong side."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Jātaka 49, Nakkhatta-Jātaka, Fausbøll i.257-259, in the English of Robert Chalmers, ed. E. B. Cowell, The Jātaka or Stories of the Buddha's Former Births, vol. I, Cambridge University Press 1895, pp. 125-126" .

pmc:TheravadaNakkhattaLowArt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The choosing of days among the low arts, and the ground is livelihood"@en, "Das Wählen der Tage unter den niederen Künsten, und der Grund ist der Lebensunterhalt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Mahāsīla of the first discourse runs seven paragraphs of the same shape: whereas some recluses and brahmans, while living on food provided by the faithful, earn their living by wrong means of livelihood, by low arts, such as these — a list — Gotama the recluse holds aloof from such low arts. Two of the seven lists carry the choosing of days. The twenty-sixth paragraph opens with arranging a lucky day for marriages in which the bride or bridegroom is brought home, arranging a lucky day for marriages in which the bride or bridegroom is sent forth, and fixing a lucky time for the conclusion of treaties of peace, for the outbreak of hostilities, for the calling in of debts and for the expenditure of money. The twenty-fourth lists the foretelling of eclipses of the moon, of the sun and of a star, of aberrations of the sun, the moon and the stars, and of their return to their usual paths."@en ;
    skos:note "Read for the falsifier of pmc:TheRefusalAndWhatItRestsOn, which named this passage as one of three places where that statement is likeliest to break, and it does deviate — but not in the way the falsifier predicted. The falsifier expected a refusal from outside grounded in doctrine alone. What stands here is grounded in neither doctrine nor case: the recurring clause is wrong means of livelihood, and the list is a list of things a recluse holds aloof from. The discourse does not say that the days do not fall out as the almanac has them. It says that one who lives on food provided by the faithful does not earn by this."@en ;
    skos:note "That is a third kind of refusal and the holding did not have it named. A refusal from outside grounded in a case argues that the art fails; a refusal from inside grounded in a state of the practitioner grants an exemption from an art that works; this one withdraws from the art without judging it, on a ground that belongs entirely to the refuser's own vocation. It is abstention and not refutation, and a protocol can take nothing from it, because nothing here is a claim about the world."@en ;
    skos:note "Two further things the passage settles for anyone who counts it. It is a list and not an argument: the seven paragraphs enumerate some two hundred items, from palmistry and snake charming to bringing forth flames from one's mouth, and the choosing of days has no special standing among them. And the enumeration is of what others do for money, so even the reading that has the discourse rejecting the art reads it as rejecting a trade."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 1, Brahmajāla Sutta, the Mahāsīla, paragraphs 21 to 27, DN i.9-12, in the English of T. W. Rhys Davids, Dialogues of the Buddha, Part I, Sacred Books of the Buddhists vol. II, London 1899, pp. 16-26; the days at paragraph 26, pp. 22-23, the constellations at paragraph 24, pp. 20-21" .

#
# Chapter XII of the handbook, read at the wording on 2026-09-24
#
# The chapter was in the holding as five nodes: the class of the powers, the
# fourteenfold mastery, the fourth absorption and two prerequisite claims. It
# carries fifteen capacities, a stated rate of attrition at seven successive
# thresholds, a clause reserving the perception of the effect to the operator,
# a lapsing time limit, two objections from a named elder against the
# prescribed technique, and an instruction to descend out of sight. None of
# that was entered. What follows is the chapter and not a selection from it;
# the five nodes above are left standing and are not restated here.
#
# Edition. The reading was done in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of
# Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, London 1931, pages 432 to 471, which is
# public domain and reachable by the regular route. The section numbers used
# in the sources below are Nanamoli's of 1956, because the five nodes already
# in this file cite by them and because Pe Maung Tin carries no section
# numbers at all; every passage was read in both. Where the two translations
# were compared the page of the one read first is given beside the section.
#

pmc:DasaIddhiyo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The ten kinds of success, with the conjuror and the ploughman among them"@en, "Die zehn Arten des Gelingens, mit dem Gaukler und dem Pflüger darunter"@de ;
    skos:definition "The handbook takes over from the Paṭisambhidā a list of ten: success by resolve, success as transformation, success as the mind-made body, success by the intervention of knowledge, success by the intervention of concentration, the Noble Ones' success, success born of the result of action, success of the meritorious, success through the sciences, and success in the sense of succeeding through right exertion in this or that work. Only the first three are reached by the course this chapter teaches. The seventh covers the flight of every bird, the eighth the riches of named householders, the ninth the conjurors who recite a spell and go through the sky and display armies in the firmament, and the tenth, on the commentary's reading, any work brought off by an art or a science, the acquiring of the three Vedas and of the three Baskets, and ploughing and sowing."@en ;
    skos:note "The word the chapter is about does not mean power. The handbook derives it from succeeding, and the tenth member is defined by that sense outright; the list is a list of things that come off, and the meditator's feats sit in it beside a good harvest. A reader who comes to this chapter expecting a catalogue of marvels has to be told that the transmission does not sort it that way."@en ;
    skos:note "The ninth member is the one that costs this corpus something, and the chapter does not notice. The conjuror who goes through the sky by a spell is admitted as a genuine member of the class; and the discourse at Dīgha Nikāya 11, which this file already holds at pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, refuses the marvel of power precisely because an onlooker may put it down to a spell of that kind. What the discourse offers as the reason for distrusting the marvel, the handbook enters as a species of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.22-39 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, London 1931, pp. 438-444; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:EkoHutvaBahu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Being one he becomes many"@en, "Einer seiend wird er viele"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first capacity of the formula. Having done the preliminary work and resolved, the meditator becomes a hundred, a thousand or a hundred thousand. The created forms resemble the creator and do whatever he does, standing, sitting, speaking or keeping silent; but if he wishes he may make them differ, some young and some old, some with shaven and some with unshaven heads, some in robes of one colour and some of another, some reciting, some preaching, some putting questions and some answering them, some dyeing, stitching and washing robes."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook states what to do when the resolve does not take: if he does not succeed by so much, he is to repeat the preliminary work, the entering and emerging, and the resolve. That sentence is the only place in the chapter where the operation is allowed to fail, and it prescribes repetition rather than any reading of the failure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.57-67 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 448-452; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:PunaEkoHoti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The many become one again when the set time runs out"@en, "Die vielen werden wieder einer, wenn die gesetzte Zeit abläuft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Having become many he becomes one again. He may do it by a second resolve; or, the handbook adds, he becomes one of himself with the lapse of the time he determined beforehand, without doing anything further."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the first of three places in the chapter where a determined period ends the effect by itself. The other two stand at the diving into earth and at the walking on water. In all three the reversion is automatic, nothing is required of the operator to close the operation, and no sign marks the end except the end."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.68 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 452; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:AvibhavaTirobhava a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He becomes visible and he becomes invisible"@en, "Er wird sichtbar und er wird unsichtbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "He lights up a dark place, reveals what is hidden, brings into focus what is out of sight; and in the other direction he turns light into darkness, the unhidden into the hidden, what is within sight into what is out of it. Others at a distance then see as he himself sees, if he so wishes; or others near at hand do not see him, and he does not see them, if he so wishes."@en ;
    skos:note "Both directions are governed by the operator's wish, and the handbook says so twice in the same paragraph. That is the same switch that stands at the diving into earth and the walking on water, here applied to seeing rather than to a surface."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.69-87 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 452-456; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:TirokuddaGamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He goes feeling no obstruction through wall, rampart and hill"@en, "Er geht ohne Widerstand durch Mauer, Wall und Berg"@de ;
    skos:definition "He enters the space device, emerges, adverts to the wall or rampart or hill, does the preliminary work and resolves that there be space; and there is space. If he wishes to go up or down there is a hollow, if he wishes to go through there is a hole."@en ;
    skos:note "A rule for two operators stands here and nowhere else in the corpus. If a hill or tree springs up on the way there is no need to resolve a second time, because a thing of the temperature order cannot arise against the strength of the first resolve; but if another possessor of the power has created it, the earlier creation is the stronger and the later comer must pass above it or below it. The transmission has thought about what happens when two of its practitioners operate on the same place, and it settles it by precedence in time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.88-91 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 457-458; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:PathaviyaUmmujjanimujja a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He dives in and out of the earth as if it were water"@en, "Er taucht in die Erde und aus ihr auf wie in Wasser"@de ;
    skos:definition "He enters the water device, emerges, marks off as much ground as he wants to become water, does the preliminary work and resolves; and the marked ground becomes water. He does not only dive: he bathes in it, drinks from it, washes his face and his clothes in it. And not water only — butter, oil, honey, molasses, whatever he chooses; and when he draws these off into jars the butter remains butter and the water water. If he wishes to be wet he is wet, and if not, not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.92-94 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 458-459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:UdakeAbhijjamaneGamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He walks on unbroken water"@en, "Er geht auf ungebrochenem Wasser"@de ;
    skos:definition "Water one sinks into when trodden on is called broken; the opposite is unbroken. He enters the earth device, emerges, marks off so much water as is to become earth, does the preliminary work and resolves; and the water in the marked place becomes earth, and he walks on it. He does not only walk: he takes whatever posture he likes, and he may make gems, gold, rock or a tree instead of earth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.95-97 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:TassaYevaSaPathavi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Only for him does the ground become water, and only for him the water earth"@en, "Nur für ihn wird der Boden zu Wasser und nur für ihn das Wasser zu Erde"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause the handbook attaches to both of the two operations that change a surface. Only for him does that ground become water; for everybody else it stays ground, and people walk on it, drive over it and plough it as before. Only for him does that water become earth; for everybody else it stays water, and fishes, turtles and water birds go about in it as they like. The restriction is not a limit on the power: if he wishes that it be water for them too, it becomes water for them too, and the same in the other direction. And when the determined time has run out, the marked extent goes back to what it was, except for water that was in pots and ponds to begin with."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a node of its own, and not as a remark on the two capacities, because of what it does to a witness. An operation that turns a road into water is the most public event a transmission could claim; this clause makes it, by default, an event that no passer-by can see, and leaves the extension of the perception to a wish of the operator's that nobody else can observe him making. A bystander who saw nothing has seen exactly what the text says he would see."@en ;
    skos:note "What it is not. It is not an account of a failure and does not stand where failures are accounted for. It stands in the operative instruction, between the resolve and the time limit, as a property of the operation; the handbook is not explaining away an absence but describing what it holds to happen. That is the difference between this clause and the family of excuses the holding collects at pmc:TheRivalWhoWasNotInTheRoom, and it is the reason this one is worth more: the excuse is made after a session, and this was written before any."@en ;
    skos:note "The wording is not weaker in the second translation. Pe Maung Tin has only for him is the ground turned into water, for others it remains ground; Ñāṇamoli has it is only for him that that earth becomes water, not for anyone else. Both carry the permission to extend it, and both carry the reversion at the end of the determined time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.94 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.97 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 459; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:AkasenaGamana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He travels cross-legged through the air like a winged bird"@en, "Er reist mit gekreuzten Beinen durch die Luft wie ein beschwingter Vogel"@de ;
    skos:definition "He enters the earth device, emerges, and marks off a place of the size he needs — of a seat if he would travel seated, of a cot if lying, of a path if on foot — and resolves that there be earth, and it becomes earth. In the sky he then walks to and fro, stands, sits and lies down."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.98-99 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 459-460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:CandimasuriyaParamasana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He touches and strokes the moon and the sun with his hand"@en, "Er berührt und streicht Mond und Sonne mit der Hand"@de ;
    skos:definition "The moon and the sun are called mighty because they move at a height of forty-two thousand leagues and light three islands at once. He may go to them and feel them; or, seated or lying here on the earth, he may resolve that they come to the side of his hand, and they come like a palm fruit cut from the stalk; or he may make his hand grow until it reaches them."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook states no device order for this one, unlike every other operation in the chapter. It is the only capacity here effected from the absorption bases alone."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only one for which the transmission records a technical question about the mechanism rather than about the procedure. A second named elder asks whether the derived matter of the hand grows and shrinks, offering as evidence that a monk who passes through a keyhole must have made it small and that one who enlarged his body must have made it large. The answer given is that the underived grows in dependence on the derived. The discussion is about how the body could do it, and nobody in it asks whether it happens."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.102-105 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 460-461; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:DureSantikeKarana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He makes the far near, the near far, the many few and the few many"@en, "Er macht das Ferne nah, das Nahe fern, das Viele wenig und das Wenige viel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four resolves of one kind, each with its named performer. A distant place is made near and a near place distant; many are made few and few many. Named beside them are two more of the same sort: a sweet thing made not sweet and a thing not sweet made sweet. The reported cases are of alms and of journeys — a river turned to cream for monks who had got nothing but dry food, an elder who contracted the earth so that seven hundred monks went to another country for their begging round and asked afterwards where the ocean had been."@en ;
    skos:note "The one restriction the handbook puts on the whole group is a statement about who is allowed to have done it: the elders who seize the distance and make it near are aged elders, and the answer to the monks who ask where the ocean went is that aged elders make the great small. The qualification is age and standing and not any state of the operator that the chapter has been teaching."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.119-135 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 465-468; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:BrahmalokeKayavasavattana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He exercises mastery with the body as far as the world of Brahmā"@en, "Er übt die Körpergewalt bis zur Brahmāwelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "He may go to the world of Brahmā with a visible body, bending the mind by means of the body so that the mind goes at the pace of the body; or with an invisible body, bending the body by means of the mind so that the body goes at the pace of the mind. In either case he descends into the perception of bliss and the perception of buoyancy, and the body becomes light as cotton wool, and he goes as an arrow goes from the bow, borne by the vehemence of the resolve."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook draws a line here that it draws nowhere else, and it is a line about what counts as having done the thing. Seeing Brahmā with the divine eye, hearing him, knowing his mind, making the far near, going to that world visibly or invisibly — of every one of these the chapter says that he has not yet exercised mastery with the body. Only when he creates a body in Brahmā's presence is the mastery exercised. The capacity is defined by its last step and not by the journey."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.119-136 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 465-469; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:VikubbanaIddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He abandons his own appearance and takes another"@en, "Er lässt die eigene Gestalt und nimmt eine andere an"@de ;
    skos:definition "Success as transformation. He discards his normal form and shows the form of a boy, a serpent, a bird, a demon, a god, a spirit, Brahmā, the ocean, a hill, a lion, a tiger, a leopard, an elephant, a horse, a chariot, a foot soldier or a whole array of an army. The handbook distinguishes the two resolves: for a shape he becomes he resolves may I be such and such a boy, and for a shape shown outside him he resolves let there be an elephant, and not may I be an elephant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.137 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 470; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:ManomayaIddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "He draws another body out of this one as a reed from its sheath"@en, "Er zieht einen zweiten Körper aus diesem wie das Rohr aus der Scheide"@de ;
    skos:definition "Success as the mind-made body, and the procedure for it. He rests from the basic absorption, adverts to the body and resolves let it be a hollow, and it is a hollow; he adverts to another body within himself, does the preliminary work and resolves, and there is another body within him; and he draws it out as a reed from its sheath, a sword from its scabbard, a snake from its slough."@en ;
    skos:note "Two limits on the created body stand in the chapter and are entered with it. It has the appearance of eye and ear and the rest but no sentient organ; and the bodies created by a disciple do only what the creator does, while only what a Buddha creates does other things and does them at his wish. The second is a rank distinction inside the capacity and not a caution."@en ;
    skos:note "The holding already carries pmc:Manomayakaya for the claim itself, from the second discourse of the Dīgha and from this chapter without a place. This node is the procedure and its limits, read at the place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.138-139 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 470-471; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmp:ParikammaAdhitthana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The preliminary work and the resolve"@en, "Die Vorbereitung und die Entschließung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operative sequence the chapter repeats at every capacity and varies only in the device used. He attains the absorption that is the basis of the direct knowledge; he emerges from it; he adverts to what is to be effected and does the preliminary work upon it, marking off the extent where an extent is in question; he attains the absorption again and emerges; and then he resolves once, by a single rising of knowledge, and what he resolved is the case simultaneously with the resolving consciousness."@en ;
    skos:note "The preliminary consciousness has the thing to be effected for its object, not a concept of it; the resolving consciousness is of the fourth absorption of the realm of form and arises once, immediately after the adoption. This is the only technical description of how the operations of this chapter are carried out, and it is the same for all of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.57-58 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 448-449; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The rate of attrition, which is the rarest thing in the chapter
#

pmc:SatesuSahassesuVaEko a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Only one among hundreds or thousands, said at seven thresholds in a row"@en, "Nur einer unter Hunderten oder Tausenden, siebenmal hintereinander gesagt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Between the statement that the fourteenfold mastery cannot be skipped and the exception made for those of great past endeavour, the handbook runs one sentence seven times over. The preliminary work of the device is a burden to a beginner, and only one in a hundred or a thousand can do it; to one who has done it the production of the sign is a burden, and only one in a hundred or a thousand can do it; and so through the extending of the sign and the attaining of absorption, the taming of the mind in the fourteen ways, the transformation by the power itself, the rapid inducing of absorption, and finally the giving of protection to another, which is the seventh and stands a little apart from the other six."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only place in the whole handbook where a rate of failure is put in numbers. The search that established that stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaAttritionElsewhereSearch: the phrase occurs seven times in twenty-three chapters and all seven are here."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is not a discouragement and does not read as one in its place. It stands in a passage arguing that the beginner must be taken through the fourteen ways like gold heated soft by a goldsmith or clay ground fine by a potter, and the two elders named in the middle of it are told to go about with their abilities perfected as soldiers go about with clean weapons. The numbers are there to say that the course is long, and the transmission is not counting anybody."@en ;
    skos:note "Why it is worth a node. A transmission that states its own rate of attrition has said something that could in principle be checked against a population, and almost nothing in this holding does. What this one says, if the seven thresholds are taken one after another as the text takes them, is smaller than any population the transmission had; and the text plainly does not mean it that way. That tension is the finding and it is not resolved here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 434-435; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.10 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 435; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:PubbayogaVisesa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Those of great past endeavour are exempt from the whole course"@en, "Wer große Vorarbeit hinter sich hat, ist vom ganzen Weg befreit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Immediately after the seven thresholds the handbook names a class for whom none of them holds. In Buddhas, in silent Buddhas and in chief disciples, who have vast previous endeavour behind them, the transformation by supernormal power and the other special qualities such as the discriminations come to success with the attainment of sanctity alone, without the course of taming the mind just described."@en ;
    skos:note "The exemption is granted on a ground that the aspirant cannot acquire, cannot verify and cannot fail to lack: what the exempt class has is endeavour in previous existences. Every member of it named by the transmission is dead and is of a rank the transmission holds to be no longer filled. The waiver therefore removes nobody from the seven thresholds who is alive to read the passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.11 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 436; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# A prerequisite the holding did not have, and it carries its own consequence
#

pmc:AkasagamanaPresupposesDivineEye a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Travel through the air presupposes the divine eye"@en, "Die Luftreise setzt das göttliche Auge voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AkasenaGamana ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Dibbacakkhu ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook gives two consequences in two sentences. Without the divine eye he does not see the hills and trees on his way, whether of the temperature order or created out of jealousy by serpents or by the fairy birds, and must meet them unprepared. And without it he cannot pick his landing place: if he comes down at an unsuitable one, at a bathing place, a landing place or a village gate, he reveals himself to the populace, and the handbook directs him to look with the divine eye, avoid such a place and descend elsewhere." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "It is the second consequence that makes this more than a traffic rule. The thing to be avoided is being seen, and the corpus that teaches the flight is the corpus in which the same act, done in front of laypeople, is an offence — see pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, and the bowl fetched from the pole in the fifth chapter of the Cūḷavagga. The handbook does not repeat the prohibition here. It supplies the technique for not being caught by it."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.100-101 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:IddhiOccultanda a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The two instructions for not being found out"@en, "Die zwei Anweisungen, nicht entdeckt zu werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter twice tells the practitioner how to keep the operation from becoming known. He is to look with the divine eye and descend where the populace will not see him. And, among the reasons for returning from many forms to one, it gives this: out of fewness of wishes he reflects that there are only a few monks in the monastery, that arriving guests would ask where so many monks resembling each other had come from, and would thus find him out; and for that reason he resolves to be one again within a fixed time."@en ;
    skos:note "Both instructions are practical and neither is a moral warning; the chapter nowhere repeats the rule of the order against display. They are entered as a caution because that is what they operate as: the thing to be guarded against is the observation of the operation by anyone who is not the operator, which is the same property that pmc:TassaYevaSaPathavi builds into the operation itself."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AkasenaGamana, pmc:EkoHutvaBahu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.68 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 452; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.101 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The dispute inside the chapter, and how it is decided
#

pmc:AkasakasinaVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the device attainment is needed at all, and what settles it"@en, "Ob die Kasiṇa-Erreichung überhaupt nötig ist, und was es entscheidet"@de ;
    pm:disputedClaim "That the operator must enter the attainment of the space device before going through a wall, and the earth device before travelling through the air, rather than resolving from mastery of the eight attainments in any device whatever." ;
    skos:definition "An elder of the same school, named in the handbook as the one who knew the three Baskets, objects twice. At the wall he asks what the use of the space device is, and argues from a case the chapter itself supplies: one who wants to create elephants and horses does not enter an elephant device or a horse device, so is not mastery of the eight attainments after the preliminary work in any device whatever the whole criterion? At the air journey he objects again, in the same words: what is the use of entering the attainment, should not his mind be concentrated, so that whatever place he resolves upon becomes space? The monks answer him the first time that the space device stands in the text and hence must certainly be so; and the handbook, having reported the second objection, closes it by saying that one should do as in the going through the wall."@en ;
    skos:note "The dispute is about technique and is settled by the location of a word. Neither side offers a case, an outcome or an attempt; the objector argues from the internal consistency of the procedure, the answer is that the procedure stands written. In a corpus whose subject is what is to be done in order to bring an effect about, this is the transmission showing what it takes to be decisive, and it is not the effect."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as a dispute and not as a note because both sides are reported, both are attributed, and the chapter is explicit about the ground on which it decides. The two sources are the two places where the same elder objects; there is no second work behind them, and none is claimed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.89 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 457; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.100 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, p. 460; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The other side, from a work of the same transmission
#

pmc:PancaAntaradhanani a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Anagatavamsa ;
    rdfs:label "The attainments go first, and the analytical knowledges last a thousand years"@en, "Die Erlangungen gehen zuerst, und die Unterscheidungswissen währen tausend Jahre"@de ;
    skos:definition "Asked about the Buddha to come, the work first describes the decline of the religion in five disappearances: of the attainments, of the method, of learning, of the symbols, of the relics. Of the first it says that only for a thousand years from the passing of the Blessed One will the monks be able to acquire the analytical sciences; then, as time goes on, the disciples will attain only to never returning, to once returning, to conversion; and with the death of the last disciple that attained to conversion the attainments will have disappeared. Of the second it says that the disciples, being unable to realise the trances, the insights, the paths and the fruits, will keep only the four purities of conduct, and then only the rule against the four deadly sins."@en ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-09-24 for one question: whether the transmission anywhere states that what the handbook teaches is no longer to be had. It does, in the only form in which such a statement can be made, by putting a date on it. The trances that the handbook makes the base of every operation of its twelfth chapter are named in the second disappearance among the things the disciples will be unable to realise."@en ;
    skos:note "What the passage does not say, and the node does not supply it. It does not name the supernormal powers. It names the analytical sciences under the first disappearance and the trances under the second, and the powers of the twelfth chapter stand on the fourth trance without being listed anywhere here. Whether the passage reaches them is an inference and is not made in this node; it is stated as an inference at pmc:IddhiAdhigamaVivada."@en ;
    skos:note "The recension is a manuscript copy and the count of years is the thing most likely to differ between copies. It is entered with the recension named for that reason, and a later reader who wants to press the date should press it there and not here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Anāgatavaṃsa, recension B of Minayeff's edition, Journal of the Pali Text Society 1886, pp. 33-34, in the English of Henry Clarke Warren, Buddhism in Translations, Harvard University Press 1896, §102, pp. 482-483" .

pmc:IddhiAdhigamaVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:Anagatavamsa ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the attainments the handbook teaches are still to be had"@en, "Ob die Erlangungen, die das Handbuch lehrt, noch zu haben sind"@de ;
    pm:disputedClaim "That a person now living who completes the training the handbook prescribes can reach the states it prescribes it for." ;
    skos:definition "Two works of one transmission, read on the same day, say incompatible things about the availability of the same attainments. The handbook sets out the whole course for a meditator in the present tense, names the seven thresholds at which most fall away and the one class exempt from them, and nowhere says that the course has lapsed or that its subject belongs to a closed age; a search of its twenty-three chapters for any such statement came back with nothing. The work on what is to come sets a term: a thousand years for the analytical sciences, then a descending series of lesser fruits, then nothing, with the trances named among the things the disciples will become unable to realise."@en ;
    skos:note "Neither work names the other and neither is answering the other; this is not a controversy the transmission conducted. What it is, and the reason it is entered as a dispute rather than as two notes, is that the two statements cannot both be acted on. A reader who takes the handbook as instruction and the other work as chronology has to decide which of them applies to him, and the transmission gives him nothing with which to decide it."@en ;
    skos:note "The step from the one to the other is an inference and is named as one here. The work on what is to come does not mention the supernormal powers. It names the trances, and the handbook makes the fourth trance the base of every operation of its twelfth chapter and the fourteenfold mastery the condition of beginning; if the trances lapse, the operations built on them lapse with them. That much follows. What does not follow is any date for the powers themselves, and none is claimed."@en ;
    skos:note "The asymmetry is worth stating because it decides where a reader should look next. A statement that the attainments have lapsed can be refuted by one attested case after the term; the handbook's silence cannot be refuted at all, because it is a silence. The search that established it stands at pmp:VisuddhimaggaDeclineSearch, and what it exhibits is a gap: in twenty-three chapters of instruction there is no sentence saying that any of it has become unavailable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1-12 (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, PTS 1931, pp. 432-436; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Anāgatavaṃsa, recension B of Minayeff's edition, Journal of the Pali Text Society 1886, pp. 33-34, in the English of Henry Clarke Warren, Buddhism in Translations, Harvard University Press 1896, §102, pp. 482-483" .
